  "query_preview": "..The theme of today's forecast is best summed up \nby the Greek philosopher Heraclitus who was attributed as \nsaying, \"nothing endures but change.\" I think this fits because\nwe will see several changes over the next several days across\nSoutheast Arizona. \n\nFirst, we are finally shifting away from the wet period we have\nbeen in the past several days where widespread showers and \nthunderstorms produced significant rainfall to the east and \nsouth of Tucson. Expect a significant downturn in thunderstorm \nactivity today as we are finally in the subsident wake of the \nupper disturbance which is currently observed on GOES water \nvapor imagery moving north through NE Arizona. In addition, the \nGOES precipitable water imagery indicated a drier airmass \n(between 1.10 and 1.25 inches of PWAT) in central Chihuahua \nMexico that will move NNW into the eastern portions of the CWA \nboth today and tomorrow...eroding the deep moisture that has \nbeen in place. The 22/00Z CAMs are all over the place with both\nthe timing and track of an MCV that is seen over extreme NW \nChihuahua early this morning. My hunch tells me we will see this\nfeature hover along the AZ/NM border as it moves north through \nthe mid-morning morning hours and this too will result in a \nsubsident wake as well. What does this all mean, I would expect \na low-grade Monsoon day with widely scattered showers and \nthunderstorms east of Tucson working off of the residual \nmoisture. \n\nThe big change with the forecast will occur the next few days as\nthe strong upper-high center over Oklahoma moves WSW into SRN \nNew Mexico by 24/00Z Thursday afternoon then into ERN Arizona \nby 25/00Z Friday afternoon. We will see afternoon temperatures \njump rapidly from a few degrees below normal today to 6-10\ndegrees above normal both Friday and Saturday. The 22/00Z NBM\ntemperatures for the Tucson International Airport (KTUS) seem a\nlittle too low with the MaxT of 106 degrees. The NBM \nprobability of 110+ degrees for KTUS is currently at 2-5% for \nboth Friday and Saturday. This contrasts with the deterministic\n22/00Z GFS and ECMWF 850-700mb thicknesses that both suggest a \nthickness between 1700-1710 m which translates via regression \nequation to to a MaxT of 110-114. Even the old-school MEX MOS \nguidance even has 111/112 FRI/SAT. I do think that given the \nsaturated soil moisture across Southeast Arizona right now, that\nwe can shave off a few degrees from this...but it may still be \nhot enough to elevate the HeatRisk across Southeast Arizona. \nGiven the uncertainty at this time, I will punt the decision on \nany extreme heat products to the dayshift.\n\nOtherwise, the heat is not the only change. The upper-high moves\ninto a more favorable location near the Four Corners Saturday \nthen NW Arizona Sunday. This opens our neck of the woods up to \na rim-shot pattern. The combination of strong surface heating, \ndrier air aloft and modest mid-level flow will increase the \npotential for stronger, more organized clustering of showers and\nthunderstorms next weekend...especially Saturday. With the \nchange, the Monsoon rolls-on.",
